course: dictionary
lesson: 12512
title: Words you can work out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some answers are not written down. You can work them out from things you already know. If an answer can be worked out, we say it is derivable. Derivable means able to be worked out, obtained or traced from something else.
explain: «The total cost is derivable from the prices on the menu.» Nobody tells you the total. Add up the prices yourself and you get it, so the total comes from the menu.
explain: Many words are derivable from smaller parts. «Happiness» is derivable from «happy», and «kindness» is derivable from «kind». Find the root word, and you can often work out what the new word means.
fact: Derivable is an adjective. It describes something that can be worked out or traced from something else.
fact: «The answer is derivable from the rules we learned today.» The answer is not given, but you can work it out from the rules.
fact: «Happiness» is derivable from «happy»: the root word happy, plus -ness, gives happiness.
fact: In maths and logic, a result is derivable if you can work it out step by step from known facts or rules.
fact: In science, a value may be derivable from measurements you have taken.
exercise: «The total cost is derivable from the prices on the menu.» What does derivable mean here? | Written in red ink | *Able to be worked out from the prices | Very expensive | Given away as a free gift
exercise: Ella says «happiness» is derivable from «happy». What does that tell you? | Happiness is a type of food | *Happiness can be worked out from the root word happy | Happy and happiness are opposites | Happy has no meaning without happiness
exercise: Which word means able to be worked out from something else? | Confusing | Lonely | *Derivable | Careless
test: «The answer is derivable from the facts we know.» What does derivable mean? | Impossible to find | Hidden under the table | Written at the back of the book | *Able to be worked out from the facts we know
test: Which word is derivable from «kind»? | *Kindness | Thunder | Pencil | Cupboard
exam: A scientist says, «The speed is derivable from the distance and the time.» What does derivable mean here? | *Can be worked out from the distance and the time | Always changing | Can't be measured at all | Shown clearly on a map
